{
  "requirement": "Repository validation MUST continue after a failed check and report every failed\ncheck it finds in one run, each as a {term}`requirements.ci.validation_error`.\nThis is a convention for every validation error; the requirement for each check\nstates which conditions that check reports.",
  "determination": false,
  "rationale": "**Verdict:** the convention is mostly implemented, but one check stops at its first failure, and the tests don't cover that case.\n\n**What works**\n- `inspect_repository_with_progress` (src/evidence.rs:170-295) collects everything into one `errors: Vec<String>`. It does not return early after a failure.\n- It runs these phases in sequence, whatever earlier phases reported:\n  - workspace config\n  - `book.toml`\n  - definitions\n  - each evidence file\n  - orphan files\n  - missing evidence\n  - reference scanning and coverage\n  - Rust citation coverage\n- The helpers also accumulate rather than stop. `collect_definitions`, `files_with_extension`, `validate_evidence_file`, `validate_input`, `validate_outline_input`, `collect_references_with_progress` and `validate_rust_functions` push an error and `continue` or move on to the next item.\n- Each error is a formatted message that starts with a path or source location, so the output is in the expected `validation_error` shape.\n- `inspect_repository` is a thin wrapper over the main function.\n- The `DefinitionKind::noun` mark in src/lib.rs only supplies wording for messages. It does no validation of its own.\n- `collects_missing_orphan_format_false_and_changed_line_errors` (src/evidence.rs:1507) puts many independent failures in one repository and asserts that all are reported together.\n- `inspection_progress_preserves_validation_and_finishes_each_phase` (src/evidence.rs:1304) shows that invalid JSON, a Rust parse error and an uncited function are all reported in the same run.\n\n**Gaps**\n1. **A check aborts at its first failure.**\n   - `terms::dependencies` (src/terms.rs, around line 108) uses `ok_or_else(...)?` when a referenced term has no definition. It returns a single `Err`.\n   - `validate_dependencies` then reports only that one \"definition dependencies\" error.\n   - Any other term problem in the same evidence file is not reported. Examples are another missing term, or the \"expected exactly one definition input for X\" check on a different term.\n   - Other validators partly cover this, such as `validate_references` for term references in source files. They do not cover every case, so the check can still stop early. That conflicts with \"report every failed check it finds\".\n2. **Config loading reports only the first failure.** `load_workspace_evidence` uses `?` throughout, and `inspect_repository_with_progress` pushes its single error (src/evidence.rs:181-183). A `habeas.toml` with several invalid fields yields one message.\n3. **The `{impl}` marking is narrow.** `requirements.ci.errors` is cited only on the orchestrator and `noun`. The continue-after-failure code in the helpers is cited only for the individual checks. This is defensible for a cross-cutting convention, but combined with the gaps above it leaves the obligation incompletely marked.\n4. **The tests miss two cases.** Nothing tests that an invalid `habeas.toml`, `book.toml` or missing-term dependency error still lets later checks run and report. The existing tests are good for evidence-schema, definition, orphan and Rust-citation failures.\n\nThere are no FIXME comments acknowledging these gaps, so the result is `false` rather than FIXME.",
